---
/**
 * The real app, one scroll below the hero.
 *
 * This is one of the six captures written into src/assets/shots by
 * `make screenshots`; the other five stay in `Screens.astro`. It is promoted
 * out of that section for the reason the section itself argues for.
 *
 * `Screens.astro` says the hand-drawn macOS mocks exist "so a reader can check
 * the drawings against it". Left at the sixth section, the thing to check them
 * against arrived four sections AFTER the drawing that most needs it: the hero
 * draws two Activity Monitor panels, four `mcp-shopify` processes against one,
 * and that drawing IS this pane's claim. It comes first now, so everything
 * after it reads as a diagram of something already seen.
 *
 * `running` rather than any of the other five, for the same reason. The hero
 * promises one process per profile and then draws it; this is the only capture
 * that photographs it — pids, uptimes, and the clients attached to each child
 * by the identity its token was issued to.
 *
 * The image is IMPORTED, not copied. It is the same file `Screens.astro` reads,
 * and `make screenshots` deletes every .png in that directory on every run —
 * parking a duplicate anywhere else would go stale on the next capture. To
 * change what this shows, change the fixture in
 * apps/apple/Bastion/DemoSeed.swift and re-run.
 *
 * `#shot-running` moved here with the figure. The anchor predates this section
 * and still resolves; the `shot-` prefix is what keeps it from colliding with
 * the bare section ids the nav uses.
 */
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import runningShot from "../assets/shots/running.png";
import SectionHeader from "./SectionHeader.astro";
---

<section class="wrap py-[96px]">
  {/* Eyebrow and heading both stay clear of `Screens.astro` further down, which
      owns "The app" over "A window, not a background process." This section is
      the narrower thing — one pane — and saying so in prose here would state
      that section's heading twice on one page. */}
  <SectionHeader
    eyebrow="The window"
    heading="What is running, and who is attached."
    class="max-w-[640px]"
  >
    <p class="m-0 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.68] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
      The panels above are drawn. This is the same claim photographed: four supervised children for
      five profiles, each with the clients currently talking to it.
    </p>
  </SectionHeader>

  {/* No heading in the caption — `SectionHeader` above carries it, and stating
      it twice on one screen reads as a mistake. */}
  <figure id="shot-running" class="reveal m-0 mt-14 scroll-mt-24">
    {/* `running` is the widest capture in the set, so it renders at the full
        column. The other five are scaled against it by `plate()` in
        `Screens.astro`; there is nothing to scale against here. */}
    <Picture
      src={runningShot}
      alt="Bastion's Running pane: prod/shopify up two hours with two clients attached, acme/keycloak up 47 minutes, home/unifi-network stopped, and lab/unifi-network with a writes badge and restarted twice."
      widths={[754, 1160, 2320]}
      sizes="(min-width: 1320px) 1160px, 100vw"
      formats={["avif", "webp"]}
      fallbackFormat="png"
      class="block h-auto w-full rounded-[14px] border border-rule-2 shadow-[0_24px_60px_-34px_rgb(0_0_0/90%)]"
    />
    <figcaption class="mt-5 max-w-[76ch] text-[14.5px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
      staging/shopify is missing a credential, so it has never started — five profiles, four
      children. Each row names its clients twice over: the identity its bearer token was issued to,
      and whatever the client calls itself, because the interesting case is when those disagree.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
